{{Japanese name|Honda}}

'''Shigehara Honda''' {{Nihongo|本多 重原|Honda Shigehara}}; (20 August 1936 – 12 May 2019) was a Sanese politician who served as the 63rd and 66th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]]. A scion of the prestigious [[Honda clan]], he was the first Prime Minister in 38 years to not belong to the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), ending the "1955 System" of single-party dominance.

The younger brother of Prime Minister [[Tadashige Honda]] (the "Showa Shogun"), Shigehara was known for a softer, more consensus-based leadership style that contrasted with his brother's authoritarianism. He served two non-consecutive terms during the turbulent early 1990s. His first term (1990–1991) was a short-lived attempt to cleanse the government of corruption following the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]]. His second term (1991–1993) was defined by the bursting of the "Go-go 8090" economic bubble and Okaiken's controversial "Checkbook Diplomacy" during the First [[Malatoan War]].

He was the uncle and political mentor of the current Prime Minister, [[Shigetsuma Honda]].

==Early life and family==
Shigehara was born in 1936 in [[Okazaki]], [[Aichi Prefecture]], fifteen years after his brother Tadashige. Due to the significant age gap, Tadashige acted as a father figure to Shigehara, especially after their father died during the [[Great Adonian War]].

While Tadashige was raised with pre-war samurai austerity, Shigehara came of age during the Allied occupation and the dawn of Sanese democracy. He attended [[Okami University]], graduating with a law degree in 1959. Unlike his brother, who viewed power as a tool for industrial construction, Shigehara viewed politics as a mechanism for social balancing.

==Political career==
He was elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in 1969, representing the [[Noto 1st District]]. He joined the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), quickly becoming a key lieutenant in his brother's faction.

When Tadashige retired from the front lines of politics in the 1980s to become the PPO's "Shadow Shogun," he groomed Shigehara to take the mantle. Tadashige believed that Shigehara's diplomatic demeanor was better suited to the complex coalition politics of the post-bubble era than his own iron-fisted style.

==Prime Minister (1990–1993)==
===First Term: The "Clean-Up" Coalition (1990–1991)===
In late 1990, the ruling LDP collapsed under the weight of the **Miyazaki Hotel Scandal**, where cabinet ministers were caught accepting bribes from developers. The LDP lost its majority, and Shigehara Honda successfully assembled a grand coalition of opposition parties, including the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), [[Komeito]], and the Democratic Action Party (DAPO).

On November 26, 1990, he became the first non-LDP Prime Minister since 1953. His cabinet was dubbed the "Clean-Up Government." However, the coalition was ideologically incoherent. When Honda attempted to pass strict anti-corruption legislation that would mandate asset disclosures for all MPs, his coalition partners—many of whom had their own skeletons to hide—revolted. The budget stalled, and the government collapsed after just four months, leading to the chaotic interregnum of [[Masaaki Fukuda]] and [[Michio Watanabe]].

===Second Term: Crisis Management (1991–1993)===
Following the collapse of the interim governments, Honda returned to the premiership in June 1991, this time leading a more stable center-right coalition (PPO-ONP-Komeito).

====The Bubble Bursts====
His second term coincided with the sudden implosion of the asset bubble. Stock prices halved, and land values plummeted. Honda's government was slow to react, initially viewing the crash as a necessary correction. By 1992, as the "Lost Decade" began to set in, his approval ratings suffered as he prioritized bailing out financial institutions over direct relief to citizens.

====The Malatoan War====
The defining foreign policy crisis of his tenure was the First Malatoan War (1991–1992). When a multinational coalition formed to expel invading forces in the Malatoa, Okaiken was pressured by [[Shayden]] to contribute. Constrained by Article 9 of the Constitution, Honda refused to send combat troops.

Instead, he pursued "Checkbook Diplomacy," pledging $13 billion to the coalition effort. This satisfied neither the international community, who mocked Okaiken's "bloodless contribution," nor domestic pacifists, who protested the use of tax money for war. In a controversial move after the cessation of hostilities, Honda dispatched Maritime Self-Defense Force minesweepers to the Persian Gulf, the first overseas deployment of the Sanese military since 1945.

===Downfall===
By early 1993, the LDP had reorganized under the savvy leadership of [[Isao Tomita]]. Capitalizing on the economic downturn and the unpopularity of the minesweeper deployment, the LDP successfully peeled away Honda's coalition partners. In February 1993, Honda dissolved the Diet but lost the subsequent election, handing power back to the LDP.

==Later life and death==
Shigehara remained a senior advisor to the PPO even after his resignation as a member and as a Prime Minister in 1993. He spent his final years mentoring his nephew, [[Shigetsuma Honda]], preparing him to retake the premiership for the family. He notably advised Shigetsuma to adopt a more decisive public persona, blending Tadashige's strength with Shigehara's tactical flexibility.

He died of pneumonia in Okami on May 12, 2019, at the age of 82.

==Legacy==
Shigehara Honda is often viewed as a transitional figure. He proved that a transfer of power from the LDP was possible, paving the way for the two-party system that would emerge in the 21st century. However, his inability to tame the bureaucracy or prevent the economic stagnation of the 1990s remains a blemish on his record.

